<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amesha Spenta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ameshaspand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, the Amesha Spenta, literally 'Immortal (which is) holy', are seven divine entities emanating from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda">Ahura Mazda</a>, through whom, in tradition, all later creation was accomplished. The doctrine is only alluded to in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a> and is set out in later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> texts such as the Bundahishn. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gathas">Gathas</a> each represents a moral quality, such as truth or good thought, that people should strive to acquire, and each has an opposing counterpart. Each is linked to one of the seven creations. The first seven days of the Zoroastrian month are dedicated to them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ahura Mazda works through the <b>Amesha Spentas</b>, the “holy immortals”, divine aspects or beings such as Good Mind, Best Truth and Holy Devotion, each linked to a part of creation, from cattle and fire to earth, water and plants.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
